   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### 7.4 Findings (exact unless stated)\n\n- **Full-contour rows.** `R_{ij}` vanishes identically exactly when `i < j`, as for gl(4). Of the 84 triples of the six remaining rows, 19 give nonzero norms for all 6 states at all 3 points and 65 give zero for all. All 19 satisfy orthogonality (30/30 cross determinants zero at P1).\n- **Gauge.** Only `\\mathcal T_a = \\{R_{11},R_{21},R_{22}\\}` and `\\mathcal T_b = \\{R_{22},R_{32},R_{33}\\}` are invariant under the residual staggering gauge. `\\hat N(\\mathcal T_b)` is `\\hat N(\\mathcal T_a)` with `10 ↔ 10̄` exchanged and the octet signs flipped.\n- **θ-dependence.** Unlike gl(4), no triple gives a θ-independent `N̂` (0 of 19 triples, for any non-vacuum state). Symbolically, for `\\mathcal T_a`:\n$$\\hat N_{10} = \\hat N_{1} = \\frac{20}{3(49-25t^2)},\\qquad \\hat N_{\\bar{10}} = \\frac{160(t^2-1)}{3(25t^2-49)},\\qquad t = \\frac{\\theta_1-\\theta_2}{h}.$$\n- **Hilbert norm (pivot gauge).** `H = 1, 2, 2, 8` for `vac, 10, 10̄, 1`, independent of `θ, h`. All eigenlines are 1-dimensional in their weight sectors, and `Ψ_R` is the highest-weight vector. The octets give θ-dependent `H` (`645/128, 86/3` at P1; `405/32, 162/25` at P2; `830/27, 249/50` at P3), because the pivot gauge is not adapted to the two-dimensional octet space.\n- **Q-side.** The gauge-invariant, `θ_1↔θ_2`-symmetric nested functions `q_1, q_{12}, q^{low}, q^{low,12}` have only trivial roots (`θ_α`, `θ_α + 2h`) and midpoint roots `(θ_1+θ_2+kh)/2`. Their values at ladder points are therefore products of `(t ± k)/2`, which cannot produce the `(7 ± 5t)` of `N̂`.\n- **Search.** With the ansatz of 7.3, 1254 × 168 × 2 candidates: **no hit** (structurally consistent with the previous point).\n\n**Status: open.** `det \\mathcal T_a` is a valid orthogonality/norm functional, but it is not a Q-dressed version of `Ψ_L·Ψ_R`. The natural next steps (not done) are: (i) state-independent linear combinations of the six rows, looking for a θ-independent `N̂`; (ii) a different row type (e.g. `u^β` insertions); (iii) octets symbolically via the rational parametrisation `t = (m-1)(m-5)/(m^2-5)`, which makes `√(4+5t^2)` rational.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  }
